1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,157
Hello everyone! We're Group
14, Team Sweet Potatoes.

2
00:00:04,157 --> 00:00:08,452
Our project explores how to help
international residents take part in local

3
00:00:08,452 --> 00:00:09,654
government decisions.

4
00:00:10,454 --> 00:00:13,612
During our investigation,
we found five problems.

5
00:00:13,612 --> 00:00:16,249
First, policy terms
are hard to understand,

6
00:00:16,249 --> 00:00:19,964
so residents lose confidence
when sharing their opinions.

7
00:00:19,964 --> 00:00:24,661
Second, it's hard to attend in-person
discussions with a busy daily life.

8
00:00:24,661 --> 00:00:28,659
Third, topics that don't feel
personally relevant get less attention.

9
00:00:28,659 --> 00:00:31,923
Fourth, residents get stuck
finding the right department.

10
00:00:31,923 --> 00:00:34,717
And last, newer residents
rely on social media,

11
00:00:34,717 --> 00:00:38,759
but official information is mostly
shared through physical channels.

12
00:00:39,559 --> 00:00:42,566
Meet Jason. He's 34, an
immigrant from Taiwan,

13
00:00:42,566 --> 00:00:45,793
and he has lived in
Melbourne for eight years.

14
00:00:45,793 --> 00:00:49,875
He has a dog and works full time,
so he can't attend council meetings.

15
00:00:49,875 --> 00:00:52,677
One morning, he finds
a flyer in his mailbox:

16
00:00:52,677 --> 00:00:55,949
the dog park will no longer
open in the mornings.

17
00:00:55,949 --> 00:01:00,474
He disagrees with the new policy, but he
doesn't know who to send his opinion to,

18
00:01:00,474 --> 00:01:02,928
or whether he's expressing it correctly.

19
00:01:02,928 --> 00:01:06,049
The last time he emailed the
council, he never heard back.

20
00:01:06,849 --> 00:01:10,919
So we introduce HearHere: a street
pillar paired with a mobile app,

21
00:01:10,919 --> 00:01:13,265
with a customised AI helper inside.

22
00:01:13,265 --> 00:01:15,253
Let's see what Jason can do with HearHere.

23
00:01:16,053 --> 00:01:19,683
On his way to the park, Jason
walks past a HearHere pillar.

24
00:01:19,683 --> 00:01:24,490
He taps the touch screen and finds the dog
park issue among the decisions near him.

25
00:01:24,490 --> 00:01:26,977
The content is shown
in Traditional Chinese,

26
00:01:26,977 --> 00:01:29,294
and difficult terms are explained simply.

27
00:01:30,094 --> 00:01:33,048
Then Jason talks to the
AI helper in Mandarin.

28
00:01:33,048 --> 00:01:35,494
Please keep the morning dog walking hours.

29
00:01:35,494 --> 00:01:38,309
It turns his opinion into
a clear English comment,

30
00:01:38,309 --> 00:01:41,119
and tells him which
department is responsible.

31
00:01:41,119 --> 00:01:44,095
Jason can check and edit
the comment before sending,

32
00:01:44,095 --> 00:01:45,927
and his voice is never recorded.

33
00:01:46,827 --> 00:01:50,155
After that, Jason taps his
phone on the NFC connector,

34
00:01:50,155 --> 00:01:52,840
and the issue moves to the HearHere app.

35
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:54,989
The pillar keeps none of his information.

36
00:01:54,989 --> 00:01:58,892
In the app, he can track his
comment: from received, to in review,

37
00:01:58,892 --> 00:02:03,387
and finally the result, with a case
number, or the reasons if it's declined.

38
00:02:04,287 --> 00:02:08,247
He can also share the issue on
Instagram, so his neighbours can join in.

39
00:02:09,147 --> 00:02:13,093
With HearHere, Jason can understand
the policy in his own language,

40
00:02:13,093 --> 00:02:16,979
find the right department, and
know his opinion has been received,

41
00:02:16,979 --> 00:02:18,864
all without attending a meeting.

42
00:02:19,864 --> 00:02:22,000
However, HearHere still has limits.

43
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,335
Speaking to a device in
public may feel uncomfortable,

44
00:02:25,335 --> 00:02:28,395
and the AI may make
mistakes when translating.

45
00:02:28,395 --> 00:02:29,358
So in the future,

46
00:02:29,358 --> 00:02:33,321
we need to test whether residents
feel comfortable speaking in public,

47
00:02:33,321 --> 00:02:35,586
whether translations keep their meaning,

48
00:02:35,586 --> 00:02:39,317
and whether people without
smartphones can still use HearHere.

49
00:02:39,317 --> 00:02:43,983
We also need to understand how the council
would manage and respond to feedback,

50
00:02:43,983 --> 00:02:46,957
so residents can trust that
their voices are heard.

51
00:02:47,857 --> 00:02:51,347
HearHere: the street pillar for
understanding local decisions.

52
00:02:51,347 --> 00:02:52,359
Speak here, be heard!
